Em ter=C3=A7a-feira, 12 de julho de 2016 13:08:51 UTC+3, la uakci escreveu:
>
> Hi!
>
> The other day, I was thinking, "wouldn't it be useful to have a=20
> sumti-raising infix cmavo?".
> In other words, wouldn't it be useful to have a cmavo that goes between=
=20
> the raised sumti and the rest.
>
> la solpa'i / la selpa'i / la me zi'o already discussed it over on his blo=
g:
> http://selpahi.weebly.com/lojban/proprietor-fronting
>
> Having said that, there is this fancy word, {zo'ei} (of LAhE).
> It's roughly equivalent to {zo'e pe}, but more of a single word than two.
> While {tu'a}, {jai} and {kai'a} (from the blog post) relate to a sumti an=
d=20
> an event,
> {zo'ei} is a relation between a sumti and something related to it.
>
> This brings us to metonyms.
>

Why is "sumti-raising" is in the topic? :)
Metonymy and raising are separate beasts.
=20

> Metonyms are somewhat related to metaphors;
> they are the usage of an object in a sentence instead of something of tha=
t=20
> object.
> For example, in the sentence "Orders came in from the office", even thoug=
h=20
> "office" really stands for "boss" (or something), "office" is used becaus=
e=20
> it has some relation to "boss".
>
> If we were to translate this sentence to Lojban with the metonym usage,=
=20
> we'd get
> { lo briju cu minde }.
> Because Lojban is a logical language to some extent, it's incorrect to sa=
y=20
> that -- offices don't give orders.
> Just like { mi cizra }, which needs a {jai} (because I'm not an event), {=
=20
> lo briju cu minde } should also be fixed with a {jai}-like cmavo, X. X,=
=20
> contrary to {jai}, would be in SE (because we don't need to use the=20
> {jai}-plus-sumtcita feature).
> In order to fix the example, we'd say {zo'ei lo briju cu minde} (which is=
=20
> not very natural), or, using X, {lo briju cu X minde}.
>
> Explaining it with a simple relation might help:
>
>  tu'a : jai : (kai'a) ::
> zoi'e :  X  :    Y
>
> It's also worth considering the Y, because it could be useful.
>
> ~ mi'e la uakci mu'o
>

